#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The original address was `https://community.ansel.photos/view-discussion/develop-photo-with-flames`.\n"
"No crawler ever captured this particular thread, so the text below — taken straight from the forum database — is the only copy left.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:27
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Posted by **cendalc** on 2024-01-15.*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:29
#, no-wrap
msgid "Hi, I'd like to get some tips how to develop photo with flames (e.g. candle). I am not able to create vibrant yellow flames. The best I could have done is this:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:33
#, no-wrap
msgid "Source RAW with my edits is on https://1drv.ms/f/s!AtyecmjANN7wna4lzFcpFDC4z_50eA?e=Jj9Bk7\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:35
#, no-wrap
msgid "Any tips/help is much appreciated!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:39
#, no-wrap
msgid "**cendalc** — 2024-01-15\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:41
#, no-wrap
msgid "For another picture, as soon as I enable Filmic, the flames are red and I don't know what to do to fix it. Here is a snapshot between enabled/disabled Filmic.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:45
#, no-wrap
msgid "Source RAW also posted to [https://1drv.ms/f/s!AtyecmjANN7wna4lzFcpFDC4z_50eA?e=Jj9Bk7](https://1drv.ms/f/s!AtyecmjANN7wna4lzFcpFDC4z_50eA?e=Jj9Bk7)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:51
#, no-wrap
msgid "Red is actually radiometrically-accurate for flames, as in : it's consistent with the lightspectrum the camera recorded. Indeed, the color temperature of flames is actually… its own burning temperature (gas burns at high temperature with blue flame, coal and candles burn lower with red flames that you don't actually see red…). See here the \"theoritical\" color of illuminants in relation with temperature (in kelvin):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:55
#, no-wrap
msgid "Filmic honors that and apply gamut mapping (aka desaturation) to fit colors into the display color space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:57
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now, what you perceptually expect here is the [Bezold-Brücke shift](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bezold%E2%80%93Br%C3%BCcke_shift), aka a yellow shift of very bright red lights, but the camera is not subjected to it because it's a product of human retina.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:59
#, no-wrap
msgid "What you see without filmic is the product of gamut clipping, where green clips earlier than red, producing a yellow shift by accident (along with ugly flat areas).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:61
#, no-wrap
msgid "The solution is a mix :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:64
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"1.  in filmic options, go for \"color science\" v7,\n"
"2.  in color balance RGB, manually push highlights to yellow with the highlights gain setting (in 4 ways tab).\n"
msgstr ""

