msgid "Im very new to Ansel as I literally found out about the software yesterday. I am however very intrigued and will likely migrate soon. Up until now I am using Darktable for my raw development and to my understanding I can do similar whitebalancing actions in both programs. Please correct me if I am wrong.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/whitebalancing-an-astromodified-camera.md:34
#, no-wrap
msgid "My problem is the following: I am mainly a landscape Photographer with a great focus on Nightscapes. I am however also a student at the university and dont have the money for two Cameras. So my one camera has to do for all my photography interests. Recently I decided to astromodify my Nikon which means I opened it up and removed a filter infront of the sensor. In essence my camera now captures a greater spectrum of light as illustrated in the following diagram.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/whitebalancing-an-astromodified-camera.md:38
#, no-wrap
msgid "After the conversion I can capture a lot more light especially in the deep reds (H-alpha emission line), which is beneficial in astrophotography but all of my photos (obviously) come out very red.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/whitebalancing-an-astromodified-camera.md:40
#, no-wrap
msgid "My question now is the following at what point do I correct this color cast? The Whitebalance Module expects the camera reference at D65, did I change that by removing the filter? I can produce similar results by changing the RGB values in Whitebalance or Colorcalibration.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/whitebalancing-an-astromodified-camera.md:42
#, no-wrap
msgid "Any ideas are welcome\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/whitebalancing-an-astromodified-camera.md:44
#, no-wrap
